-- title:
--   Only two cusps on X₀(ℓ)_ℚ̄ at prime level
-- statement:
--   Let $\ell$ be a prime and let $\bar F_\ell$ denote `modularFunctionFieldBar ℓ`, the intermediate field of $\bar{\mathbb Q}((q))$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$ generated by the coefficientwise image under `coeffEmb` of `modularFunctionFieldFull ℓ` (itself the subfield of $\mathbb Q((q))$ generated over $\mathbb Q$ by the set `divisorExpansions ℓ`). Write $\bar j$ for the element of $\bar F_\ell$ obtained by applying `coeffEmb` to the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{ofPowerSeries}(jNumQ)$, i.e. the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$. Let $w$ be a place of $\bar F_\ell$ over $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, that is, a valuation subring of $\bar F_\ell$ which is not the whole field, contains the image of $\bar{\mathbb Q}$, and is a principal ideal ring. Assume $\bar j$ is not in the valuation subring of $w$, which is the meaning of `IsCusp` for $\bar j$ and $w$. Then $w$ equals either `cuspInftyBar ℓ`, the place whose valuation subring consists of the elements of non-negative $q$-order, or `cuspZeroBar ℓ`, its translate under the Fricke involution `frickeInvolutionBar ℓ`.
--
--   This is the classical statement that $X_0(\ell)$ over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero has exactly two cusps, $\infty$ and $0$, here in the form: any place of the function field at which $j$ has a pole is one of these two. It is used throughout the analysis of places of $\bar F_\ell$ and their specialisations, in particular by the level-one prolongation lemmas on divisors and residues.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_eq_cuspInftyBar_or_eq_cuspZeroBar.lean

